Fix offline never-opened error-block: in /app/frontend/app/job/[id].tsx, add explicit handling for the offline sentinel. Suggested patch: check (q.error as any)?.offline first and short-circuit to the exact copy; also split the error branch from the !q.data branch so React Query's loading state is not mis-rendered as error. Example:
   const err: any = q.error;
   if (err?.offline) return <OfflineEmpty title="NOT AVAILABLE OFFLINE" sub="OPEN THIS JOB ONCE WHILE CONNECTED." />;
   if (q.isError) return <ErrorBlock message={err?.message || 'Failed to load job data'} onRetry={q.refetch} />;
   if (!q.data) return <Loading />;

rca_of_issue:
Reproduction: login as installer, open job A online (cached), context.set_offline(True), SPA-push to /job/{000000000000000000000000}. Expected ErrorBlock 'NOT AVAILABLE OFFLINE. OPEN THIS JOB ONCE WHILE CONNECTED.' (from offlineCache.ts:34 which throws Object.assign(new Error('...'), {offline: true})). Observed: ErrorBlock shows 'FAILED TO LOAD JOB DATA' (fallback). Investigation: the condition at /app/frontend/app/job/[id].tsx:21 is `if (q.isError || !q.data)` — either (a) the sentinel error.message gets stripped by React Query 5 before reaching q.error (so q.error is non-null but .message is empty), or (b) !q.data triggers before q.isError does because with networkMode:'offlineFirst' and no retry, the query's isLoading flips to false while status remains 'pending' (data undefined, error null). Mitigation (recommended patch, apply to BOTH /app/frontend/app/job/[id].tsx AND /app/frontend/app/project/[id].tsx):
```ts
const err = q.error as any;
if (err?.offline) return <View style={s.root}><ScreenHeader title="JOB" back /><View style={{padding: space.lg}}><ErrorBlock message="Not available offline. Open this job once while connected." /></View></View>;
if (q.isError) return <ErrorBlock message={err?.message || 'Failed to load job data'} onRetry={() => q.refetch()} />;
if (!q.data) return <Loading />;
```
This makes the offline sentinel explicit (no reliance on React Query preserving e.message) and separates the loading/no-data branch from the error branch so the pending state is never shown as an error.
